## 1. The objective, quoted in full (idea-repo#1)

> Joint-embedding predictive architectures (JEPAs) learn representations by predicting the embedding of one part of an input from the embedding of another part, with no negative examples and no reconstruction loss. They are prone to representation collapse, in which the learned embeddings degenerate to a constant or to very low rank. In practice collapse is avoided with a collection of empirical heuristics (how the predictor is designed and scaled, how much of the input is masked, how the target encoder is updated), and no single account explains why they work. The insight to build on: collapse is decided early in training, near the collapsed solution, where two effects compete in every direction of representation space, a driving force that grows the direction and a decay effect that shrinks it. Whether a direction survives, and therefore how much rank the representation can develop, depends on which of the two is stronger there. The research problem is to turn this insight into a theory that explains and predicts when JEPAs collapse and what the common heuristics change, and to test that theory empirically.
>
> Deliver a complete paper that could be submitted to NeurIPS 2026, written to the venue's submission requirements: the LaTeX source in the NeurIPS template, the compiled PDF, and the code and experimental results that the paper's claims rest on.
>
> Do not use any paper, preprint, code release or other written work first published after 1 September 2026.

Supplied material: none beyond the issue text (no data files, no related-work
list, no code). Everything else the study uses must be built or obtained from
public sources dated on or before 2026-09-01.

## 2. Requirements (numbered so positions can cite them)

R1. **Theory.** Turn the stated insight into a theory: near the collapsed
    solution, in every direction of representation space, a *driving force*
    (grows the direction) competes with a *decay effect* (shrinks it); a
    direction survives iff the driving force is stronger there. The theory
    must be stated precisely (model, assumptions, linearisation around the
    collapsed solution, the per-direction growth/decay quantities and the
    survival condition) with proofs or derivations.
R2. **Explain *when* JEPAs collapse.** The theory must give a criterion that
    predicts complete collapse (no direction survives) vs. dimensional
    collapse (few survive) vs. healthy rank, i.e. a prediction of the
    reachable rank from quantities of the data and the training set-up.
R3. **Explain what the common heuristics change**, each named in the
    objective, in terms of driving force vs decay:
    - R3a predictor design and scaling (e.g. presence, depth, width,
      learning rate / initialisation of the predictor);
    - R3b how much of the input is masked (masking ratio / strategy);
    - R3c how the target encoder is updated (EMA momentum / schedule,
      stop-gradient).
    Weight decay is the natural explicit "decay" knob and should be covered
    too, but it is not named in the objective; the three named heuristics
    are mandatory.
R4. **Predict, not only explain.** At least some claims must be forward
    predictions (e.g. a threshold or phase boundary, a predicted rank)
    stated before and checked against experiments.
R5. **Empirical test.** Test the theory's predictions experimentally: in a
    setting where the theory is exact or near-exact (to validate the
    derivation) and in real JEPA training with neural encoders (to show the
    predictions transfer), varying each heuristic of R3. Results must report
    uncertainty (seeds) and the measured quantities the claims rest on
    (spectra / effective rank, collapse vs. not, downstream quality).
R6. **Deliverable: a complete NeurIPS 2026 paper** written to the venue's
    submission requirements (owned by `venue-repo`; e.g. anonymised
    submission, page limit, checklist): LaTeX source in the official
    NeurIPS 2026 template, the compiled PDF, and
R7. **the code and experimental results** the claims rest on, delivered with
    the paper (reproducible: scripts, configs, raw result files, the inputs
    each figure/table is made from).
R8. **Temporal constraint.** No paper, preprint, code release or other
    written work first published after **2026-09-01** may be used: not
    cited, not read for ideas, not used as code. Every citation in the paper
    needs a verifiable first-publication date on or before 2026-09-01; code
    libraries must be versions released on or before that date (pin them).
    Datasets/standard benchmarks are fine if they were public before then.

## 3. What the study must produce (derived from R1-R7)

- A formal model of JEPA training near collapse (the collapsed solution and
  its linearisation), the per-direction driving force and decay, the
  survival condition, and the resulting rank prediction.
- One analysis per heuristic (R3a-c, plus weight decay) saying which side of
  the competition it changes and how, with a testable consequence (a phase
  boundary, a scaling, a sign).
- Experiments: (i) a solvable model (e.g. linear/deep-linear JEPA on
  synthetic data with controlled spectra) where predicted and measured
  survivors/rank are compared quantitatively; (ii) neural JEPA training on a
  standard image benchmark varying predictor, mask ratio, EMA (and weight
  decay), comparing predicted vs measured collapse/rank, with seeds.
- Figures/tables a reader can check the claims against: predicted vs
  measured phase diagrams/boundaries, per-direction growth over early
  training, singular-value spectra / effective rank over training,
  downstream (linear-probe) quality where relevant.
- The paper, code and results per R6-R7.

## 4. Conventions to follow (field practice; details in Section 5.5)

- Collapse measured on embedding singular-value spectra and an effective
  rank (RankMe-style entropy rank; Roy & Vetterli 2007), plus embedding
  std; downstream quality by linear probe (and/or k-NN).
- Comparison against the established theory of non-contrastive SSL
  dynamics (BYOL/SimSiam/DirectPred eigenmode analyses, deep-linear JEPA
  analyses) so novelty is positioned correctly.
- Hyper-parameters of the heuristics reported in the units practice uses
  (EMA momentum tau, mask ratio, predictor depth/width, weight decay).

## 5. Prior work map (what is established; all dated <= 2026-09-01)

### 5.1 Closest prior theory (the paper must position against these)
1. **Tian, Chen & Ganguli, ICML 2021 (arXiv 2102.06810), DirectPred.** Linear
   BYOL/SimSiam, eigen-aligned modes; on the invariant parabola
   p_j' = p_j (tau p_j - (1+s^2) p_j^2 - eta). The drive (tau p_j) is
   *quadratic* in the mode near the origin, so with any weight decay eta>0 the
   collapsed point is *linearly stable*; a mode survives only if it starts
   outside the basin p_j > p*_- , and all collapse if eta > tau^2/(4(1+s^2)).
   Covers EMA (tau, beta), predictor lr ratio alpha_p, weight decay; no
   masking, predictor depth/width, isotropic data, symmetric predictor.
   **Reviewers will read any drive-vs-decay claim against this paper.**
2. **Arbel, Terver & Ponce 2026, PEIRA (2605.17671)** and **Ponce et al. 2025
   (2507.01028).** Stop-grad + optimal ridge predictor + norm penalty lambda:
   canonical correlation c survives iff c^2 >= 4 lambda; total collapse is
   stable; EMA stability left open. Equilibrium analysis, no masking/predictor
   scaling.
3. **Ziyin, Lubana, Ueda & Tanaka, ICLR 2023 (2210.00638).** "Stability at the
   origin decides collapse": per-subspace linear criterion (signal vs
   augmentation + weight decay) — but only for contrastive/decorrelation
   losses (no predictor, stop-grad, EMA).
4. **Littwin et al., NeurIPS 2024 (2407.03475).** Deep linear JEPA with
   stop-grad: per-feature ODE w' = lambda w^{3-1/L} - lambda w^3/rho; greedy in
   regression coefficient rho; no weight decay, EMA or masking, so nothing
   collapses.
5. **Wang, Chen, Du & Tian 2021 (2110.04947).** DirectSet/DirectCopy: a feature
   is discarded iff its augmentation variance s^2 > 1/(4 eta) - 1.
Also: Simon et al. ICML 2023 (stepwise SSL: linear growth rate at origin, step
time ~ log(1/init)/rate, modes with non-positive rate never grow); Halvagal et
al. NeurIPS 2023 (predictor eigenvalues as per-mode rate multipliers
lambda(1-lambda); no stop-grad -> all collapse); Tang et al. ICML 2023 and
TD-JEPA 2025 (optimal/fast predictor conserves Phi^T Phi: no growth, no
collapse); Bao 2023 (cosine loss changes the drive/decay balance); Zhuo et al.
ICLR 2023 (rank differential); Wen & Li 2022 (predictor rescues weak
features); Richemond et al. 2023 (edge of orthogonality); Jing et al. ICLR 2022
(dimensional collapse in contrastive SSL).

### 5.2 What is not covered (the gap the paper can fill)
- G1. No *linear-stability* (first-order) per-direction criterion at the
  collapsed point for JEPA/self-distillation. In Tian/Wang/Bao/PEIRA the drive
  is quadratic at the origin. A first-order drive arises if the predictor is
  O(1) at the collapsed point (e.g. predictor not collapsed at init): linearising
  w_i' = p_i c_i wbar_i - (p_i^2 a_i + eta) w_i, wbar' = beta (w - wbar) gives
  survival iff p_i c_i > p_i^2 a_i + eta (independent of beta). This is a
  suggestion recorded in `refs/lit_tools.md`, not a prior result. The paper
  must say which regime it treats and reconcile with Tian's basin picture;
  otherwise its novelty over Tian 2021 is unclear.
- G2. No theory of how predictor **width/depth/scale/initialisation** moves the
  per-direction threshold (prior work: lr ratio, closed-form or optimal
  predictors only).
- G3. No collapse-dynamics theory of the **masking ratio** (prior masking
  theory is about MAE optima/identifiability; useful moment identities:
  Bisulco et al. 2025, Dong et al. NeurIPS 2025 — masked covariance
  (1-m)^2 Sigma + m(1-m) diag/blkdiag Sigma; masked-PLS BBP threshold scaling
  rho^{-1/2}, Gjolbye et al. 2026).
- G4. **EMA** enters only qualitatively ("curriculum", Tian) or is left open
  (PEIRA); scaling rule rho -> rho^kappa under batch scaling (Busbridge et al.
  NeurIPS 2023).
- G5. Prior thresholds assume isotropic/commuting data or optimal predictors;
  none is tested against reachable rank in realistic (I-JEPA-style) training.
- G6. Empirically, no paper ablates EMA/no-EMA or sweeps predictor
  depth/width/lr or mask ratio **against rank** for image JEPAs (I-JEPA
  reports accuracy only; LiDAR reports I-JEPA RankMe rises then falls).

### 5.3 Tools the theory can build on
Small-init deep-linear dynamics (Saxe et al. 2014: mode growth rate ~ signal,
time ~ log(1/eps)/s; Li, Luo & Lyu 2021: near 0 the flow is power iteration
U' = -grad f(0) U, adding weight decay lambda gives growth iff mu_i > lambda;
Jacot et al. 2021: escape time ~ alpha^{-(L-2)} for depth L>2; Atanasov et al.
2022 silent alignment). Weight decay as rank threshold (nuclear-norm identity;
Kobayashi et al. NeurIPS 2024: L2 on a factorised map = nuclear norm on the
product, so the predictor∘encoder singular values are soft-thresholded; Ziyin,
Li & Meng NeurIPS 2022: zero is the global minimum iff ||E[xy]||^2 <=
gamma_u gamma_w; Wang & Jacot 2023). EMA/target networks as two-timescale
systems (Tian 2021; Fellows et al. ICML 2023 for TD). Equations: `refs/lit_tools.md`.

### 5.4 What practice establishes about each heuristic (numbers in `refs/lit_practice.md`)
- Stop-grad: required (SimSiam without it 0.1%; BYOL tau=0 -> 0.3%).
- EMA: BYOL ImageNet top-1 tau=1 (frozen random target) 18.8, 0.999 69.8,
  0.99 72.5, 0.9 68.4; DINO without momentum collapses; data2vec: tau too low
  -> collapse. I-JEPA/DINO/BYOL schedule 0.996->1; V-JEPA 2 constant 0.99925.
- Predictor: removal collapses BYOL/SimSiam; fixed random predictor fails;
  BYOL without EMA needs predictor lr multiplier >= 2 (1x -> 5.5%, 2x -> 62.8%);
  I-JEPA depth 6->12 +2.9 (IN-1%), width 384 better than 1024 (bottleneck);
  SimSiam hidden d/4 more robust.
- Masking: I-JEPA multi-block 54.2 vs random 17.6; target scale peaks at
  (0.15,0.2); 1 target block 9.0 vs 4 blocks 54.2; V-JEPA ~90% ratio best
  ("low coverage -> trivial prediction task").
- Weight decay: I-JEPA ramp 0.04->0.4; BYOL wd=0 unstable; Tian: large eta
  forces collapse.
- Rank: in I-JEPA, RankMe rises early then falls; best accuracy is not at max
  rank (LiDAR, Thilak et al. ICLR 2024).

### 5.5 Conventions to follow for comparability
- Rank: RankMe (Garrido et al. ICML 2023; exp-entropy of normalised singular
  values of the N x K embedding matrix, eps ~ 1e-7, ~25.6k samples, compare
  within a method only); optionally LiDAR (Thilak et al. 2024) for JEPAs;
  sorted log singular-value spectra of the centred embedding covariance on
  held-out data (Jing et al. 2022) at several checkpoints; SimSiam std of
  l2-normalised outputs vs 1/sqrt(d).
- RankMe as published is computed on the singular values of the *raw*
  (uncentred) embedding matrix; a plan that uses centred embeddings must say
  so (centring removes the constant "collapsed" component, which is what a
  JEPA collapse theory needs, but it is not RankMe verbatim).
- Per-direction dynamics are reported in prior theory as: eigenvalues of the
  embedding covariance (or of W W^T) vs training time on a log scale, showing
  stepwise growth (Simon et al. 2023, Fig. 1-2); predictor vs encoder
  eigenvalue alignment and p_j(t) trajectories with the predicted fixed
  points p*_+- (Tian et al. 2021); per-feature amplitude w_i(t) with
  predicted critical times (Littwin et al. 2024). Phase diagrams of collapse
  vs (weight decay, other knob) with the analytic boundary overlaid are the
  natural figure for thresholds (Tian 2021 shows basins in (p, s) plane).
- Quality: frozen linear probe (primary), k-NN monitor; mean +- std over >= 3
  seeds. At small scale, logistic regression on frozen, pooled features is
  an accepted cheap probe (simple-ijepa: sklearn LogisticRegression, STL-10);
  solo-learn trains an online/offline linear head with SGD on frozen
  features. Report which probe and its training budget.
- Small-scale benchmarks used by theory papers: CIFAR-10/100, STL-10
  (ResNet-18), ImageNet-100 / ImageNette (ViT-T/S). Expect low absolute I-JEPA
  numbers at small scale (ViT-T 100 ep IN-1K linear 22.1%, C-JEPA Table 9);
  reference baselines: solo-learn BYOL/SimSiam numbers.
- Heuristic grids that mirror practice: EMA tau in {0, 0.9, 0.99, 0.996,
  0.999, 1}; predictor depth/width/lr multiplier; mask ratio / target scale /
  number of blocks; weight decay {0, fixed, ramp}; report collapse as phase
  diagrams of measured rank vs predicted boundary.
- Reusable MIT-licensed code: lightly (I-JEPA modules), galilai-group/
  stable-pretraining (RankMe/LiDAR, I-JEPA benchmarks), solo-learn;
  facebookresearch/ijepa is CC BY-NC (research use OK; state it). Pin
  versions released <= 2026-09-01.

### 5.6 Which candidate predictions of the plan are already established (answers story-repo request idea-repo#2)
Plan v0.2 (story-repo `plan.md` section 2) uses the linear model
w_k' = p c_k wbar_k - (p^2 sigma_k + lambda) w_k with growth rate
g_k(p) = p c_k - p^2 sigma_k - lambda. Mapping to prior notation: Tian 2021's
Delta_j = tau p - (1+s^2) p^2 - eta is the same polynomial with c <-> tau
(target scale x cross-covariance, X = I) and sigma <-> 1+s^2 (context
variance incl. augmentation noise); Littwin 2024's regression coefficient
rho_i = lambda_i/sigma_i^2 is c_k/sigma_k; Halvagal 2023's
lambda_m(1-lambda_m) is g at c = sigma = 1, lambda = 0.

| Plan item | Status in prior work | What is new for the paper |
|---|---|---|
| P1 static survival c^2/(4 sigma) > lambda | **Established in form**: Tian 2021 (all modes collapse iff eta > tau^2/(4(1+s^2))), Wang et al. 2021 (feature dropped iff s^2 > 1/(4 eta) - 1), PEIRA 2026 (c^2 >= 4 lambda, canonical correlations). All for augmentation noise, isotropic/aligned data, quadratic-drive regime. | Per-direction anisotropic c_k, sigma_k from masking; the first-order drive regime (predictor O(1) while encoder small). In Tian's invariant WW^T = alpha_p^{-1} W_p^T W_p + e^{-2 eta t} C this regime is the *unbalanced* initialisation (C large), which Tian sets aside by analysing the parabola C -> 0. Say so explicitly. |
| P2 rank = escaping directions; race with predictor decay; at lambda = 0 ordering by growth rate | Ordering/stepwise learning established: Simon et al. 2023 (step time ~ log(1/init)/rate), Littwin 2024 (JEPA greedy in rho = c/sigma), Saxe 2014, Jacot 2021. Basin picture with weight decay: Tian 2021 (survive iff p_j > p*_-). | The race criterion (integrated growth vs log(1/w_0) along the decaying predictor trajectory), computable from data statistics + init scales + hyperparameters, and its test as a *rank* predictor in neural JEPAs: not in prior work. |
| P3 no stop-grad -> all decay | **Established**: Tian 2021 (no SG: vec(W)' = -H vec(W), H PSD), Halvagal 2023 (rate -(1-lambda)^2), Ponce et al. 2025 (linear case without SG/EMA always collapses); empirically SimSiam (0.1%), BYOL tau=0 (0.3%). | Only the per-direction Cauchy-Schwarz form is a re-derivation; present as consistency, cite. |
| P4 no predictor fails when c <= sigma + lambda; growth window 0 < p < c/sigma; LR/init/scale move p through the window | Partly established: window = Halvagal's lambda_m(1-lambda_m) (closed-form predictor; no predictor -> no learning); Tian: larger predictor LR ratio shrinks collapse basin, too large stops growth; empirical BYOL App. I (no-EMA needs predictor LR x2+; collapse if predictor lr <= projector lr), SimSiam (no/fixed predictor fails); Wen & Li 2022 (predictor rescues weak features). | Predictor **init scale, width, depth** as an effective gain moving p through the window, and the predicted transition location: new (gap G2). Empirical anchors: I-JEPA depth 6->12 better, width 384 > 1024. |
| P5 masking sets c_k/sigma_k; rank falls with mask ratio | **Not established** (gap G3). Tools: masked moment identities (Bisulco et al. 2025; Dong et al. NeurIPS 2025), masked-PLS BBP scaling (Gjolbye et al. 2026). Empirics: I-JEPA target scale optimum (0.15,0.2), 1 vs 4 blocks 9.0 vs 54.2; V-JEPA best ~90%; LiDAR: I-JEPA rank sensitive to mask scale. | New. Caution: with disjoint Bernoulli context/target masks the cross-covariance scales like m(1-m) (non-monotone in m) while context variance falls like (1-m); check that "rank falls monotonically with ratio" survives, and note practice (V-JEPA) finds high ratios best for accuracy, not rank. |
| P6 EMA changes rate, not linear threshold | Not stated as such. Related: Tian 2021 (slower EMA enlarges collapse basin; "curriculum"), PEIRA (EMA has the same equilibria as SG, stability open), Ponce 2025 (SG/EMA equilibria asymptotically stable). Busbridge et al. 2023 EMA scaling rule (rho -> rho^kappa) is a time-scale statement, consistent with P6. Empirics: BYOL tau sweep (tau=1: 18.8, 0.99: 72.5), DINO/data2vec. | New as a linear-stability statement; must reconcile with Tian's basin-enlargement result (EMA acting through the race). |
| P7 weight decay raises threshold; stepwise rank in lambda | **Established in form** (Tian, Wang 2021, PEIRA; contrastive: Ziyin et al. 2023; deep linear: Ziyin, Li & Meng 2022; L2 on factorised map = nuclear-norm soft threshold: Kobayashi et al. 2024). | Stepwise rank at lambda = c_k^2/(4 sigma_k) with mask-dependent c_k, sigma_k and its test in neural JEPAs. |
| LeJEPA (2511.08544, 2025-11-11) | Date qualifies. Explicit isotropic-Gaussian regulariser (SIGReg), no EMA/SG/predictor needed; makes collapse impossible by construction, no dynamics theory. | Compare only as the "explicit regulariser" alternative. |

Novelty statement the evidence supports: a unified per-direction early-time
drive-vs-decay account for JEPAs that (i) covers predictor scale/init/width/
depth, masking ratio and EMA in one criterion, (ii) predicts reachable rank
from data statistics and set-up, and (iii) is tested on neural JEPAs. The
static threshold, the necessity of stop-grad and the weight-decay threshold
must be credited to Tian 2021 / Wang et al. 2021 / PEIRA 2026 / Halvagal 2023.

## 6. Not established / open, and the cutoff warning

- **Post-cutoff work exists on exactly this question.** Literature searches
  surface at least one arXiv preprint posted after 2026-09-01 that appears to
  address JEPA collapse as drive vs decay (arXiv 2610.02344), plus others
  (2609.23881, 2609.09630). They were not read and must not be read, cited or
  used by any position (R8). When searching, check the v1 date before opening
  anything; ids 2610.* and later are out, 2609.* need the date check. Novelty is judged against
  the pre-cutoff work in Section 5 only.

- The objective fixes no dataset, architecture or compute budget; these are
  plan decisions (`story-repo`) subject to feasibility (`idea-review-repo`).
- The objective does not name which "decay effects" are in scope; weight
  decay, EMA lag and predictor-induced shrinkage are the candidates.
- Venue specifics (2026 template, page limit, checklist, anonymity) are
  established by `venue-repo`, not here.

